1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,045
We've built a shared understanding of opportunities,

2
00:00:03,045 --> 00:00:05,084
we've defined the desired outcomes,

3
00:00:05,084 --> 00:00:08,085
and we've sketched a whole bunch of different ideas.

4
00:00:08,085 --> 00:00:09,960
Now it's time to decide.

5
00:00:09,960 --> 00:00:11,970
For this phase of the design sprint,

6
00:00:11,970 --> 00:00:15,060
the goal's to converge on a single idea to carry forward for

7
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:19,380
the remainder of the sprint all the way through prototyping and user validation.

8
00:00:19,380 --> 00:00:25,500
In this lesson, we'll cover decision making methods to get consensus on one idea,

9
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:30,240
with a focus of making sure that the idea solves a real need for real users.

10
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:34,865
Sometimes this can be challenging because people might be attached to their own ideas,

11
00:00:34,865 --> 00:00:37,965
but it's important to make sure that the team is

12
00:00:37,965 --> 00:00:41,270
evaluating ideas using the same subjective criteria,

13
00:00:41,270 --> 00:00:45,980
like impact, timing, and feasibility when considering different ideas.

14
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:51,170
Specifically, we'll discuss a couple of methods to help facilitate some of these topics.

15
00:00:51,170 --> 00:00:55,670
One of those methods is assumptions and questions to make sure that we're challenging,

16
00:00:55,670 --> 00:00:58,415
thinking, and not overlooking anything.

17
00:00:58,415 --> 00:01:01,775
Another method is creating a decision matrix

18
00:01:01,775 --> 00:01:05,360
in order to compare ideas against value and effort.

19
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:08,105
Finally, we'll talk about thinking hats

20
00:01:08,105 --> 00:01:11,120
to make sure that different perspectives are covered.

21
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:13,230
But before we get started,

22
00:01:13,230 --> 00:01:17,420
it's important that the team has a solid understanding of all the different ideas.

23
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:19,415
An easy way to do this for

24
00:01:19,415 --> 00:01:23,315
each team member to share and present their solutions sketches one-by-one.

25
00:01:23,315 --> 00:01:27,615
Each team member should take a couple minutes to present their solutions sketch,

26
00:01:27,615 --> 00:01:30,860
walking the group through their idea frame-by-frame.

27
00:01:30,860 --> 00:01:33,829
If other team members have questions about the idea,

28
00:01:33,829 --> 00:01:37,430
they can ask specific questions after the sketch has been presented.

29
00:01:37,430 --> 00:01:39,980
But keep in mind that your question should be

30
00:01:39,980 --> 00:01:43,380
more focused on understanding the idea at this point.

